   "source": [
    "## 4. Fit on the training years and predict\n",
    "\n",
    "Each model is fitted once on the fit set (80% of 2002-2004) with its chosen settings. For the fit set itself I also keep **out-of-fold** predictions (leave-one-year-out), so there is an honest in-time view on 120,000 loans and not only on the small hold-out. They are also what notebook 04 will use to fit recalibration."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "id": "0e86aefa",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-11-16T17:55:54.241620Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-11-16T17:55:54.241620Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-11-16T17:56:25.484293Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-11-16T17:56:25.484293Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "fitted 6 models\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "models, preds = {}, {}\n",
    "eval_sets = {**tests}\n",
    "for name, cols in variants.items():\n",
    "    kind = \"logistic\" if name.startswith(\"logistic\") else \"boosting\"\n",
    "    Xf = get_X(kind, fit, cols)\n",
    "    models[name] = build(kind, cols, best[name]).fit(Xf, y_fit)\n",
    "    out = {\"2002-04 out-of-fold\": cross_val_predict(build(kind, cols, best[name]), Xf, y_fit, groups=g_fit, cv=logo,\n",
    "                                                   method=\"predict_proba\")[:, 1]}\n",
    "    for sname, d in eval_sets.items():\n",
    "        out[sname] = models[name].predict_proba(get_X(kind, d, cols))[:, 1]\n",
    "    preds[name] = out\n",
    "print(\"fitted\", len(models), \"models\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 7,
   "id": "584d8d00",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-11-16T17:56:25.484293Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-11-16T17:56:25.484293Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-11-16T17:56:25.835819Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-11-16T17:56:25.835819Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "saved predictions: (300000, 10)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# keep every prediction for notebook 04 (not tracked by git)\n",
    "frames = []\n",
    "for sname, d in {\"2002-04 out-of-fold\": fit, **eval_sets}.items():\n",
    "    f = pd.DataFrame({\"loan_id\": d[\"loan_id\"].to_numpy(), \"vintage\": d[\"vintage\"].to_numpy(), \"set\": sname,\n",
    "                      \"y\": d[\"default_24m\"].to_numpy()})\n",
    "    for name in variants:\n",
    "        f[\"p_\" + re.sub(r\"[^a-z]+\", \"_\", name)] = preds[name][sname]\n",
    "    frames.append(f)\n",
    "pred_table = pd.concat(frames, ignore_index=True)\n",
    "pred_table.to_parquet(ROOT / \"data\" / \"processed\" / \"predictions.parquet\", index=False)\n",
    "print(\"saved predictions:\", pred_table.shape)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a706f89f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. How well do they do?\n",
    "\n",
    "For every model and test set: ranking (AUC), the average predicted default rate against the actual one, and the Brier score and log loss. Brier skill is the improvement over a model that always predicts the training default rate. The 95% intervals come from resampling the loans 200 times, so a small difference between two models should not be read as real unless the intervals are well apart. (The intervals do not account for loans of the same year sharing the same economy, so they are a little too narrow.)"
   ]
  },
