   "source": "## 7. What I see\n\n**Ranking (AUC)**\n- In-time, both model types rank well: AUC about 0.85 to 0.87 on the out-of-fold predictions and the hold-out.\n- Out of time it falls steadily but stays decent. For the plain logistic model: 0.81 (2005), 0.79 (2006), 0.77 (2007). Boosting: 0.83, 0.78, 0.77. So the models still tell safer loans from riskier ones in the crash years, only less sharply.\n- Boosting is a bit better in-time and in 2005 (0.83 against 0.81), but in 2006 and 2007 the two are indistinguishable (the intervals overlap).\n\n**Calibration (predicted against actual default rate)**\n- In-time the models are right on average: the ratio of predicted to actual is 0.92 to 1.02, and the 95% intervals contain 1.\n- Out of time they are far too low, and getting worse. For the logistic model the ratio is 0.74 in 2005, 0.42 in 2006 and 0.18 in 2007. The model predicts about 0.8% to 0.9% in every one of those years, while the actual rate climbs from 1.05% to 1.94% to 4.90%. The predicted rate hardly moves, so the model captures almost none of the shift.\n- Boosting predicts a little more in 2007 (1.04%), so a small part of the rise is visible through the borrower features, but it is still about five times too low (ratio 0.21).\n- So the pattern in the problem statement is there: ranking stays fairly good while the probabilities become wrong. This is one stress episode in one dataset, so it is not proof that it always happens.\n\n**Brier score and log loss**\n- The Brier skill (improvement over always predicting the training default rate) is tiny everywhere, 0.02 to 0.03, and slightly negative for the logistic model in 2005 (-0.014). With defaults this rare the Brier score is dominated by the base rate, so it hides the problem that the ratio shows clearly. Log loss rises from 0.034 in-time to 0.224 in 2007, but it is not comparable between years because the default rate differs.\n\n**The variants**\n- Removing the year-linked features (interest rate, loan term, loan purpose) changes the AUC very little and lowers the predicted rates slightly. It does not fix the calibration.\n- State matters for boosting out of time. With `state`, boosting has AUC 0.769 in 2007; without it (and without the regions) 0.789, and the intervals do not overlap (0.761-0.777 against 0.781-0.797). `state` carries 17% of the boosting gain, so the model leans on state effects learned in the calm years, and they do not carry over. For logistic regression, state or region makes no difference.\n- This comparison was planned in advance, but I looked at it on the test years. Choosing a variant because of it would be tuning on the test set, so it is an observation, not a decision.\n\n**What the models rely on**\n- Credit score is the main driver in both (43.9% of the boosting gain; the largest numeric coefficient in the logistic model). Then state (boosting), number of borrowers, rate spread, interest rate and mortgage insurance percentage.\n\n**Cautions**\n- One crash in one dataset, and the 24-month window of a 2006 or 2007 loan lies inside the downturn.\n- The in-time hold-out has only 209 defaults, so its intervals are wide. The intervals do not account for loans of one year sharing the same economy, so they are a little too narrow.\n- These are baselines. I did only a small search of settings, and I did not try to maximise the score.\n- The detailed calibration work (reliability curves, slope and intercept, and whether recalibration helps) is in notebook 04."
